 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "york_ivory_bangle_lady",
   "title": "The Ivory Bangle Lady",
   "text": "In 1901 a stone coffin was dug up on Sycamore Terrace: a wealthy woman buried in the late 300s with ivory and jet bangles and a blue glass jug. In 2010 researchers found she probably had North African ancestry. Roman York was a mixed city; the emperor Septimius Severus, born in what is now Libya, died here in 211.",
   "people": "romans"
  },
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 7,
   "text": "THE CITY WALLS. About two miles of medieval wall round the old city, the longest town walls in England, and you can walk most of the way on top. The gates are called 'bars', and the streets are called 'gates'. It's York."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "YORK MINSTER. Begun in 1220 and finished 252 years later. The Great East Window is the size of a tennis court, the biggest stretch of medieval stained glass in Britain. The stonemasons have been repairing it ever since."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "THE CRYPT. Under the Minster lie Saxon, Norman and Roman stones, and a Viking burial. Locals tell the story that the Keeper of the cathedral's quiet places holds court below. If you find the door, say good morning."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "MULTANGULAR TOWER. A Roman tower from the fortress of Eboracum, with later medieval stones on top. Constantine the Great was proclaimed emperor here in AD 306, which is why York's coffee mugs say 'Rome's Backup Capital'."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "THE SHAMBLES. A medieval street of butchers' shops, named for the 'shamel', the bench where meat was laid out. The upper floors lean out so far that neighbours across the lane can nearly shake hands."
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 52,
   "text": "CLIFFORD'S TOWER. A 13th-century stone keep on the mound of William the Conqueror's wooden castle. A royal fortress, a prison, a gunpowder store and a very good view. The mound is not natural. Someone dug it."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 60,
   "text": "KNAVESMIRE. York Racecourse, a huge flat meadow that floods in winter, a good place for an afternoon, and once the site of the northern gallows. Today, thankfully, it only gallops."
  },
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Guy Fawkes of the Gunpowder Plot was born in Stonegate in 1570 and baptised at St Michael le Belfrey.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "york_guy_fawkes",
   "title": "Guy Fawkes"
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 15,
   "plaque": "york_name",
   "title": "Name: York",
   "text": "The Brittonic name Eburacon probably meant 'place of yews'. The Romans made it Eboracum, the Angles heard 'eofor', boar, and made it Eoforwic, and the Vikings turned that into Jorvik. Say Jorvik quickly with a heavy cold and you have York."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 59,
   "plaque": "york_mallard",
   "title": "Mallard",
   "text": "In 1938 the streamlined steam locomotive Mallard reached 126 mph on a long downhill stretch near Grantham, a world speed record for steam that still stands. She lives in the museum here, blue and gleaming, looking fast even when parked."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "york_chocolate",
   "title": "The Chocolate City",
   "text": "York ran on cocoa. Rowntree's gave the world the Kit Kat and the Aero, and Terry's made the Chocolate Orange here. Joseph Rowntree, a Quaker, built the village of New Earswick for working families, with proper gardens, because a good cocoa deserves a good home."
  },
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 17,
   "plaque": "york_merchants",
   "title": "Merchant Adventurers",
   "text": "Built in the 1350s by a religious guild, this timber hall later became home to York's Company of Merchant Adventurers, who traded cloth and much else overseas. Its great oak frame has stood for more than 650 years. The floor slopes a little. So would yours."
  },
  {
   "x": 69,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "FINGERPOST. East: the Yorkshire Wolds, Rudston and Hull. Chalk hills, big skies and a city with cream phone boxes."
  }
 ],
